          <th scope="col" class="p-3 border-b border-hairline font-semibold">Estimated ABV</th>
          <th scope="col" class="p-3 border-b border-hairline font-semibold">Total Calories (kcal)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-hairline"><td class="p-3">Light Beer</td><td class="p-3">12 oz (355 ml)</td><td class="p-3">~4.2%</td><td class="p-3">95 - 110 kcal</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-hairline"><td class="p-3">Standard Lager/Ale</td><td class="p-3">12 oz (355 ml)</td><td class="p-3">~5.0%</td><td class="p-3">140 - 160 kcal</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-hairline"><td class="p-3">Craft IPA / Double IPA</td><td class="p-3">12 oz (355 ml)</td><td class="p-3">7.0% - 9.0%</td><td class="p-3">200 - 300+ kcal</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-hairline"><td class="p-3">Dry Red Wine (e.g., Cabernet)</td><td class="p-3">5 oz (148 ml)</td><td class="p-3">~13.5%</td><td class="p-3">120 - 130 kcal</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-hairline"><td class="p-3">Dry White Wine (e.g., Pinot Grigio)</td><td class="p-3">5 oz (148 ml)</td><td class="p-3">~12.0%</td><td class="p-3">110 - 120 kcal</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-hairline"><td class="p-3">Straight Spirits (Vodka, Gin, Tequila)</td><td class="p-3">1.5 oz (44 ml)</td><td class="p-3">40.0% (80 proof)</td><td class="p-3">~97 kcal</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-hairline"><td class="p-3">Margarita (Traditional)</td><td class="p-3">8 oz (236 ml)</td><td class="p-3">Varies</td><td class="p-3">250 - 400 kcal</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-hairline"><td class="p-3">Piña Colada</td><td class="p-3">8 oz (236 ml)</td><td class="p-3">Varies</td><td class="p-3">450 - 600 kcal</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Evidence-Based Strategies for Drinking on a Diet</h2>
  <p class="text-body mb-4">
    Incorporating alcohol into a calorie-restricted diet requires strategic planning. The primary goal is to minimize the caloric surplus while mitigating the negative metabolic effects of ethanol on recovery and muscle protein synthesis. Consider these expert strategies:
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-body space-y-3 mb-8">
    <li><strong>Prioritize Straight Spirits:</strong> Opt for clear spirits mixed with calorie-free options like soda water, ice, or diet tonic. This isolates the calories to the ethanol itself, completely eliminating sugar.</li>
    <li><strong>Implement Caloric Buffering:</strong> If you plan to consume 400 calories worth of alcohol, reduce your carbohydrate and fat intake earlier in the day by 400 calories. Do not reduce your protein intake, as protein is vital for satiety and muscle preservation.</li>
    <li><strong>Hydration Protocol:</strong> Consume one full glass of water between every alcoholic beverage. This not only slows down your drinking pace but also helps counteract the diuretic effect of alcohol, preventing dehydration-induced hunger the following day.</li>
    <li><strong>Track Relentlessly:</strong> Treat a beer exactly as you would treat a slice of pizza. Enter every drink into your nutrition tracker. Awareness is the greatest tool for prevention of caloric overspill.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid the "Drunchies":</strong> Alcohol lowers inhibitions and directly stimulates the hypothalamus, increasing appetite. Pre-plan a high-protein, fibrous meal for after your drinking session to prevent uncontrolled binge eating on highly palatable, calorie-dense foods.</li>
  </ul>

